     1	# Native dependency packaging seam
     2
     3	`TASK-260715-1g9cyt` establishes the extension-safe packaging boundary used by
     4	HEV/lwIP and either SSH candidate. The checked-in harmless fixture exercises the
     5	binary packaging verifier from a test-only evidence target; it is not part of a
     6	production adapter, provider, or harness graph.
     7
     8	## Decision
     9
    10	ADR-019 selects a static XCFramework SwiftPM `binaryTarget` for custom-build C
    11	graphs. HEV is not a practical SwiftPM C-source target: its pinned build merges
    12	the tunnel, core, task-system, lwIP, and yaml archives, applies platform-specific
    13	sources and flags, and is already verified upstream as an XCFramework. A local
    14	source build retains those semantics and gives one inspectable static archive
    15	per Apple slice. Dynamic libraries, runtime downloads, `dlopen`, private
    16	frameworks, and unchecked vendor binaries are prohibited.
    17
    18	This is not permission for opaque binary updates. Every artifact update must
    19	include the pinned revision and source hash, compiler flags, target triples,
    20	license inputs, rebuild command, and per-file artifact hashes in
    21	[`NativeDependencies/manifest.json`](../NativeDependencies/manifest.json).
    22	The checked-in source or pinned upstream graph is the review authority. CI
    23	rebuilds the artifact and requires byte-identical file hashes before accepting
    24	the binary diff.
    25
    26	Swift dependencies whose upstream package graph is itself the reviewed source,
    27	such as a possible `ReluxNIOSSH` fork, stay normal pinned SwiftPM source
    28	dependencies. They do not need an XCFramework merely for uniformity.
    29
    30	## Target graph
    31
    32	```text
    33	ReluxTunnelCore                       (no native or platform dependency)
    34	        ^
    35	        |
    36	ReluxTunnelNativeAdapter ----------> HevSocks5Tunnel binaryTarget
    37	        ^                                      |
    38	        |                                      `-- static XCFramework only
    39	        +-- ReluxTunnelIOSAdapter
    40	        +-- ReluxTunnelMacOSAdapter
    41	        `-- ReluxTunnelHarnessSupport --> ReluxTunnelHarness
    42
    43	ReluxTunnelNativeAdapterTests -----> CReluxNativeFixture binaryTarget
    44	                                     (evidence only)
    45	```
    46
    47	The HEV integration now occupies `ReluxTunnelNativeAdapter` itself: the adapter
    48	depends on Core contracts plus the pinned `HevSocks5Tunnel` binary target and
    49	supplies the bridge's real scoped-borrow consumer. A possible
    50	`ReluxTunnelLibSSH2Adapter` occupies the same layer. Provider and harness
    51	composition roots retain the named adapter dependency; `ReluxTunnelCore` and
    52	its public contracts do not change direction. A `ReluxNIOSSHAdapter` uses the
    53	same layer but depends on the pinned Swift source package instead of a binary
    54	target.
    55
    56	## Rebuild and verification
    57
    58	The harmless test-only fixture is rebuilt from the manifest inputs with:
    59
    60	```sh
    61	./scripts/native-dependency-tool.py build-fixture \
    62	  --output NativeDependencies/Artifacts/ReluxNativeFixture.xcframework
    63	make validate-native
    64	```
    65
    66	The build creates iOS device arm64, iOS Simulator arm64/x86_64, and macOS
    67	arm64/x86_64 static archives. All C compilations use `-fapplication-extension`.
    68	The validator checks the plist slice declarations against the archives, rejects
    69	missing architectures, non-static binaries, dynamic-loader symbols and load
    70	commands, absolute build paths, missing module maps, source-hash drift, artifact
    71	hash drift, and stale notices. Xcode builds run with
    72	`APPLICATION_EXTENSION_API_ONLY=YES` for both provider destinations. Linked
    73	release products are stripped of debug/source tables before signing and the
    74	linked-binary audit; the audit rejects any remaining absolute checkout path or
    75	non-system dynamic dependency. The generated Xcode package scheme proves the
    76	universal macOS compile/link configuration; the linked-product audit uses the
    77	non-instrumented SwiftPM release harness because Xcode's generated package
    78	scheme injects test-coverage paths that are not part of production packaging.
    79
    80	The pinned HEV graph is rebuilt only from a disposable recursive checkout:
    81
    82	```sh
    83	./scripts/native-dependency-tool.py build-hev \
    84	  --source-dir /path/to/pinned/hev-socks5-tunnel \
    85	  --output NativeDependencies/Artifacts/HevSocks5Tunnel.xcframework \
    86	  --notices NativeDependencies/Generated/HEV_THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.txt
    87	```
    88
    89	Before invoking upstream `build-apple.sh`, the command verifies the root and all
    90	four submodule revisions and deterministic git-archive SHA-256 values from the
    91	manifest. The build seam then renders the verified script in memory so every
    92	iOS/tvOS build uses the manifest's 18.0 minimum and every macOS build uses its
    93	15.0 minimum; the pinned checkout remains unchanged. Missing, unknown, or
    94	unmodeled Apple SDK entries fail before compilation. The wrapper also fixes static
    95	archive timestamps through `ZERO_AR_DATE`, so clean rebuild hashes are stable.
    96	After compilation it performs
    97	the same static/slice/extension-safety inspection, verifies the per-file artifact
    98	lock, and emits notices directly from the verified license files into both the
    99	generated evidence location and a generated static Swift notice API (avoiding
   100	SwiftPM's absolute build-path resource fallback). The Apple matrix
   101	builds the native adapter and both provider configurations for iOS device, iOS
   102	Simulator, and macOS, then proves that the stripped harness contains HEV's
   103	`main_from_str`, `quit`, and `stats` symbols.
   104
   105	At runtime the adapter generates HEV configuration in memory, lends only the
   106	bridge-owned endpoint B to one dedicated pthread, calls the public unmodified
   107	quit API, joins that pthread, and leaves descriptor closure to bridge cleanup.
   108	Its SOCKS side binds only IPv4 loopback and requires fresh per-run RFC 1929
   109	credentials before an accepted channel can reach the injectable adapter seam.
   110	The pending-authentication limit and timeout remain caller-owned inputs.
   111
   112	## Cache policy
   113
   114	The optional build cache root is named by `RELUX_NATIVE_CACHE_DIR`; when unset,
   115	automation may use `relux-native-dependencies` below the process temporary
   116	directory. Cache keys include dependency, revision, source hash, Xcode build,
   117	and compiler flags. Cache contents are never authoritative: source verification
   118	and artifact inspection always run on a hit. A mismatch deletes only that keyed
   119	entry and then rebuilds. No dependency is downloaded or loaded at application
   120	runtime.
   121
   122	## Candidate plug-in checklist
   123
   124	For a new custom native graph such as libssh2:
   125
   126	1. Add the exact upstream graph, source hashes, licenses, flags, and slices to
   127	   the manifest.
   128	2. Add a fail-closed verifier/build recipe and generated notice hook.
   129	3. Inspect the static XCFramework and lock its per-file hashes.
   130	4. Add a named adapter target depending on Core plus the binary target.
   131	5. Run provider, harness, archive, architecture, and extension-safety gates.
   132
   133	For ReluxNIOSSH, pin the source revision and package checksum/identity, place its
   134	concrete implementation in a named SSH adapter, and run the same provider and
   135	harness matrix. ADR-014 still owns the SSH engine selection; this seam does not
   136	pre-decide it.
   137
   138	The source fork now lives as the independent package
   139	`Dependencies/ReluxNIOSSH`, pinned and diff-verified against SwiftNIO SSH
   140	`0.14.1` / `31cdc3c3391a10460dedf1170530cf651d2ca496`. It deliberately keeps
   141	the `NIOSSH` product/module name for source compatibility. The later
   142	`ReluxNIOSSHAdapter` target may add it as a package dependency; no
   143	`ReluxTunnelCore` target depends on it.
   144
   145	The libssh2 candidate uses the C side of the same seam. `ReluxLibSSH2` is a
   146	static XCFramework binary target rebuilt from checksum-verified libssh2
   147	`a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` plus OpenSSL `3.5.7`. The source
   148	tool verifies both archives before extraction or patching, applies the single
   149	six-file allowlisted observation patch in a disposable tree, and merges static
   150	libssh2 and libcrypto archives per architecture. It then enforces the existing
   151	architecture, minimum-OS, static-link, forbidden-symbol, extension-safety,
   152	absolute-path, public-header, notice, and artifact-lock gates. Its stable
   153	logical OpenSSL prefix prevents task-local build paths from entering runtime
   154	metadata.
   155
   156	```sh
   157	make build-libssh2 \
   158	  LIBSSH2_SOURCE_ARCHIVE=/path/to/libssh2-a343024.tar.gz \
   159	  OPENSSL_SOURCE_ARCHIVE=/path/to/openssl-3.5.7.tar.gz
   160	make validate-libssh2
   161	```
   162
   163	The named `ReluxTunnelLibSSH2Adapter` target is attached only to the macOS
   164	provider and harness products. It is intentionally absent from
   165	`ReluxTunnelCore`, the shared native adapter, and every iOS product. The adapter
   166	imports only the XCFramework's public module and headers. Fork provenance,
   167	exact delta, and rebase policy live under
   168	[`Dependencies/ReluxLibSSH2`](../Dependencies/ReluxLibSSH2/README.md).

