     1	# Relux Tunnel
     2
     3	Relux Proxy **v2** — a native macOS and iOS **full-tunnel system VPN** whose
     4	external transport is one or more authenticated **SSH** connections to a
     5	user-controlled host. To the OS it is a system VPN; on the wire it is SSH to
     6	your own machine.
     7
     8	This repository is the home of the v2 product. The legacy menu-bar SOCKS app
     9	(v0.1.0) lives in [`relux-works/relux-proxy`](https://github.com/relux-works/relux-proxy)
    10	and remains buildable until the migration task explicitly retires it. Its
    11	[preservation contract](docs/legacy-preservation.md) pins the independent
    12	SwiftPM, defaults, system-SSH, bundle, packaging, and release identity.
    13
    14	## Architecture (one glance)
    15
    16	```
    17	NEPacketTunnelFlow
    18	      ↕  public AF_UNIX/SOCK_DGRAM packet bridge (no utun-FD discovery)
    19	HEV + lwIP  (userspace TCP/IP, low-memory tuned)
    20	      ↕  internal SOCKS5 (component boundary, not a user proxy)
    21	SSHProxyAdapter
    22	      ↕  2–4 pinned SSH lanes
    23	remote sshd  →  relux-relay (rootless, exec/stdio)  →  Internet
    24	```
    25
    26	- **TCP** rides SSH `direct-tcpip` (local termination — no TCP-over-TCP).
    27	- **UDP/DNS** ride a rootless `relux-relay` uploaded over the same SSH session.
    28	- **Degraded mode**: TCP + safe DNS-over-TCP when the relay is unavailable.
    29	- **Full spec**: see [`.spec/`](.spec/) — start with `.spec/README.md`,
    30	  `architecture.md`, `decisions.md` (ADR log), and `threat-model.md`.
    31	- **Shared package**: `ReluxTunnelCore` and its compile-only iOS/macOS provider
    32	  composition roots include the supervised public socket-pair packet bridge,
    33	  bounded deterministic v1 runtime configuration/message codecs, and the
    34	  injectable owned `NETunnelProviderManager` repository plus a generation-safe
    35	  host session controller whose system status and provider capability authorities
    36	  remain separate behind thin public-API host seams. Core also contains the
    37	  injectable, non-waiting TCP handshake/flow/open/queued-byte admission registry
    38	  and its fixed-cardinality aggregate diagnostics; the module boundaries are mapped in
    39	  [`docs/core-adapter-boundaries.md`](docs/core-adapter-boundaries.md).
    40	- **Native dependencies**: pinned custom-build C graphs use source-rebuilt
    41	  static XCFrameworks behind `ReluxTunnelNativeAdapter`; see
    42	  [`docs/native-dependency-packaging.md`](docs/native-dependency-packaging.md).
    43
    44	## macOS experiment harness
    45
    46	`ReluxTunnelHarness` is a standalone SwiftPM command-line target for fast
    47	packet, SSH, relay, fault-injection, and metrics experiments without a Network
    48	Extension lifecycle or generated workspace. Its support module composes the
    49	same `ReluxTunnelCore` runtime contracts used by the providers.
    50
    51	The initial stable subcommand is `smoke`. It accepts a versioned JSON document
    52	from a file or inline, exercises temporary-file, Unix-socket, and managed-task
    53	cleanup, then writes one sorted-key JSON result to standard output:
    54
    55	```bash
    56	swift run ReluxTunnelHarness smoke --configuration ./smoke.json
    57	```
    58
    59	```json
    60	{
    61	  "schemaVersion": 1,
    62	  "seed": 42,
    63	  "sourceRevision": "replace-with-git-revision",
    64	  "dependencyRevisions": {},
    65	  "profileReference": {"value": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111", "privacy": "sensitive"},
    66	  "parameters": {
    67	    "mode": {"value": "noop", "privacy": "public"}
    68	  }
    69	}
    70	```
    71
    72	Configuration values marked `sensitive` are emitted as `<redacted>`. The output
    73	records result and metric schema versions, source/dependency revisions, seed,
    74	redacted configuration, duration, platform, and metrics. `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM`
    75	cancel the active command and exit with codes 130 and 143 after cleanup.
    76
    77	## Planning and execution
    78
    79	Work is tracked on a file-based board in [`.task-board/`](.task-board/), driven
    80	by the `task-board` CLI (project-management skill). Six delivery epics
    81	(M0–M5) hold agent-autonomous work; a seventh epic,
    82	`manual-validation-and-approvals`, isolates every human-in-the-loop step
    83	(physical-device validation, Apple portal actions, App Review, legal/product
    84	decisions, credential ceremonies) so the delivery epics run without stalling an
    85	agent loop.
    86
    87	- Board overview: `task-board q --format compact 'summary()'`
    88	- Plan a milestone: `task-board q 'plan(EPIC-… , mode=children)'`
    89	- Planning snapshots: [`.planning/`](.planning/)
    90	- **Spawn policy** (serial Codex/Sol orchestrator, Claude/Opus 5 producer,
    91	  independent Codex/Sol reviewer, lite context):
    92	  [`docs/spawn-policy.md`](docs/spawn-policy.md)
    93
    94	## Distribution
    95
    96	- **macOS**: Developer ID + hardened runtime + notarization + stapling, stable
    97	  `ReluxProxy.dmg` from authenticated GitHub releases, plus the accepted
    98	  **in-app self-update design**: exact Sparkle 2.9.4, a public HTTPS signed feed,
    99	  EdDSA-signed final DMGs that are already Developer ID signed/notarized/stapled,
   100	  and a separately activated packet-tunnel system extension. Implementation and
   101	  physical update evidence remain release gates. See ADR-018,
   102	  `.spec/platform-distribution.md`, and
   103	  [the dated decision](.research/260721_macos-self-update.md).
   104	- **iOS**: Apple Distribution via TestFlight/App Store; the App Store owns iOS
   105	  updates.
   106
   107	## CI
   108
   109	`ci.yml` runs board + spec structural validation with no Apple credentials on
   110	every push/PR. The full quality-gate pipeline (build matrix, signing/notarization
   111	verification, relay reproducibility, SBOM/secret scanning, serialized release
   112	orchestration) is built out under the M5 CI story.
   113
